# Test: a subscriber closes IPCP after session-up, and only IPv4 goes and comes back.
# QUESTION:  when a subscriber's IPCP is closed by a Terminate-Request after the
#            session came up, does ze answer it, withdraw the IPv4 family from the
#            session's pppN and from the subscriber registry while the session
#            stays up, and install it again when IPCP reopens, with no idle close
#            at the no-NCP bound?
# VALIDATES: spec-ppp-ncp-independent-lifetime AC-4 (IPv4 form: the frame is read
#            from the unit fd, the Terminate-Ack carries the request's
#            Identifier, the address leaves pppN, `show subscriber id <id>
#            detail` stops printing ipv4), AC-6 (b) (the no-NCP bound armed when
#            IPCP left Opened is cancelled by the reopen: LCP echo still
#            answered and no CDN past the bound) and AC-8 (IPCP renegotiates
#            on the held address, the address and ipv4 return). User story 3,
#            on ze's IPv4-only pool, the only pool ze ships.
# PREVENTS:  an IPCP close after up that leaves the registry announcing an
#            address the subscriber no longer holds (the release event never
#            reaching the transport), an IPCP close that kills the whole
#            session, and a reopen that leaves the idle bound running.
#
# Why not a unit test: TestNCPTerminateAfterSessionUpOnUnitFD drives the PPP
# session over a fake unit fd and a test bus. Only the daemon joins the kernel's
# ppp_generic, which delivers an NCP frame after session-up to the unit fd and
# not the channel, ze's unit reader, the PPP release event, the L2TP reactor,
# the subscriber bridge and the CLI. So only a .ci shows an operator the
# registry following one family.
#
# Every assertion is on state, never on elapsed time, but one. The peer waits
# until `show subscriber` lists the session active with its address, `show
# subscriber id <id> detail` prints ipv4, and pppN holds an IPv4 address: those
# are the positive controls, so an empty field after the Terminate-Request is
# not a broken command. Each later wait polls the same reads. The one horizon
# is the no-NCP bound itself: an idle close is an event that does NOT happen,
# so the peer keeps the session up until 3 seconds past `ncp timeout` measured
# from ze's Terminate-Ack (the bound was armed before that Ack left ze), fails
# on any LCP Terminate-Request, CDN or StopCCN in that span, and then requires
# an answer to an LCP Echo-Request.
#
# The two short timers are the test's, set below. `lcp restart-timer 500` is
# the Restart time IPCP spends in Stopping after the Terminate-Ack, before a
# Configure-Request can reopen it, so the reopen falls well inside the bound.
# `ncp timeout 15` is that bound. The peer's second argument repeats 15: the
# peer cannot read ze's config, and a mismatch shortens or lengthens only the
# hold, which a red would show.
#
# Revert walk: remove the `s.publishNCPRelease(family)` call in the LCPActTLD
# case of performNCPAction (internal/component/l2tp/ppp/ncp.go). The address
# still leaves pppN (withdrawNCPFamily runs either way), but no release reaches
# the subscriber bridge, so the wait for `show subscriber id <id> detail` to
# print no ipv4 fails.
#
# A session that reaches an assigned IPv4 needs kernel PPPoL2TP, which needs
# CAP_NET_ADMIN, so this test is needs-linux and runs in QEMU as root. It SKIPS
# on an unprivileged dev host. ze.l2tp.skip-kernel-probe is deliberately NOT
# set, as in test/l2tp/radius-acct-wire.ci, whose LAC half and RADIUS mock this
# test reuses (internal/test/fixture/tunnel_fixture_l2tp_ppp.go,
# tunnel_fixture_l2tp_radius.go). The peer runs in ze's network namespace, so
# it reads pppN's addresses from the kernel directly.
#
# Ports: $PORT is the L2TP listener, $PORT2 the RADIUS mock (auth and
# accounting), $PORT3 the daemon's SSH server, which the peer's `ze cli`
# reaches through the three ze.ssh.* variables below.

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
    authentication {
        # bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
        user admin {
            password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
        }
    }
}
l2tp {
    enabled true
    shared-secret s3cr3t
    auth-method chap-md5
    lcp {
        restart-timer 500
    }
    ncp {
        timeout 15
    }
    auth {
        radius {
            nas-identifier lns1
            timeout 5
            retries 2
            server mock {
                address 127.0.0.1
                port $PORT2
                accounting-port $PORT2
                shared-key testing123
            }
        }
    }
    pool {
        ipv4 {
            gateway 10.99.31.1
            start 10.99.31.10
            end 10.99.31.10
        }
    }
}
environment {
    l2tp {
        server main {
            ip 127.0.0.1
            port $PORT
        }
    }
    ssh {
        enabled true
        server main {
            ip 127.0.0.1
            port $PORT3
        }
    }
}
EOF_CONF

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp:value=debug
option=env:var=TEST_SECRET:value=s3cr3t
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT3

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test fixture l2tp/ppp-ncp-terminate-after-up/radius $PORT2 testing123
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=config
expect=stderr:contains=l2tp: PPP session up
# 120s bounds the peer's waits, each a tenth of this budget, and its hold of
# about 18s past the Terminate-Ack, so a stall ends with the peer naming the
# state that never came. The happy path takes about 25s.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=le test fixture l2tp/ppp-ncp-terminate-after-up $PORT 15:timeout=120s:exit=0
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK: CHAP Success received
expect=stdout:contains=OK: IPCP opened, ze assigned 10.99.31.10
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: show subscriber lists an active l2tp subscriber with address 10\.99\.31\.10 on ppp[0-9]+
expect=stdout:contains=OK: show subscriber id detail prints ipv4 10.99.31.10 before the IPCP Terminate-Request
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: ppp[0-9]+ holds IPv4 address [0-9.]+ before the IPCP Terminate-Request
expect=stdout:contains=OK: ze answered the IPCP Terminate-Request with a Terminate-Ack carrying Identifier 90
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: ppp[0-9]+ holds no IPv4 address after the IPCP Terminate-Request
expect=stdout:contains=OK: show subscriber id detail prints no ipv4 after the IPCP Terminate-Request, the subscriber still active
expect=stdout:contains=OK: IPCP opened again, ze assigned 10.99.31.10
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: ppp[0-9]+ holds IPv4 address [0-9.]+ again after IPCP reopened
expect=stdout:contains=OK: show subscriber id detail prints ipv4 10.99.31.10 again after IPCP reopened
expect=stdout:contains=OK: ze answered an LCP Echo-Request 18s after the IPCP Terminate-Ack, past the 15s no-NCP bound, with no LCP Terminate-Request and no CDN
